    "island": "st_thomas",
    "aliases": [],
    "description": "Waterlemon Cay; Islet, 30 feet high, area 118 square rods, 260 yards west of Lelnster Point, at east side of Leinster Bay entrance, northern shore of St, John. Described in the Derrotero as, \"Cayuelo Acantilado\" (steep little Cay), because of its bluff shore, except for a sand spit making out 80 yards sautheost. Overgrown with brush and grass. Named by Oxholm, \"Water Limon Kay, \" spelled by Knox (p. 223), \"Waterlemon\"; from a plant there 80 called, the Pae&fiwa taurifoUa, which beam a bright yellow, finely flavored fruit, known as \"Bellapple. \" Chartmakers converted Waterlemon \" into the more familiar \" Watermelon \" : this last trandated by the Spanish into \"Sandfa, \" better \"Zandla. \" Local name, \"Jewel Cay. \" G. B. , November 7, 1923, restored original name. G. P. \"Jewel\": lat. 18\" 22' 08. 6\" (261 m. ), long. 64\" 43' 24\" (709 m. ).",
